COMMON PITFALLS TO AVOID
========================
{{~ if !model.handle_names.empty? ~}}

DO NOT forget to dispose {{ for name in model.handle_names }}{{ name }}{{ if !for.last }}, {{ end }}{{ end }}.
Each owns a native object; use `using` (Dispose is idempotent). After Dispose
every other call throws ObjectDisposedException. A consuming method (its XML
docs say so) uses the object up: afterwards every call except Dispose throws
InvalidOperationException.

DO NOT share one object between threads expecting parallel speed. The native
object is not thread-safe; the wrapper serialises calls on one instance with a
lock.

DO NOT expect a copy to write through. Get<Field>() getters, and methods whose
Rust function returns a reference, return a COPY (their XML docs say so):
changing the copy does not change the object it came from.
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if !model.is_pass_through ~}}

DO NOT pass text in another encoding. Strings go to the crate as UTF-8 with an
explicit length (no NUL terminator needed) and come back decoded from UTF-8.

DO NOT expect status codes. Every failure (a Rust error, an argument the
native side rejects - a length, invalid UTF-8 -, a panic inside the crate)
throws {{ model.exception_class_name }}; its message is the Rust text and its
Status tells which: -1 null argument, -2 an argument the native side rejected,
-3 an error the crate returned, -4 a panic inside the crate (that object is
broken afterwards: only Dispose is allowed), -5 an object already consumed.
{{~ end ~}}

DO NOT run on a runtime identifier that is not listed above. The first call
then throws DllNotFoundException naming the expected file.


